/*
  Symbolic model of the four-phase PUF-based UAV authentication protocol.
  Tamarin prover, multiset-rewriting / Dolev-Yao.

  Run:  tamarin-prover --prove formal/tamarin/uav_puf_auth.spthy

  WHAT IS PROVED HERE, AND WHAT IS NOT
  -------------------------------------
  This model carries the *protocol-level* claims of the security analysis:
  mutual authentication in Phase 2 and Phase 3, replay resistance, session-key
  secrecy, perfect forward secrecy, and physical-capture isolation. It does not,
  and cannot, carry the computational claims, and the paper says so:

   - The PUF is idealised as a private per-device function. Concretely, a device
     holds a secret `si` and puf(si, ch) is the response. The function symbol is
     public, so the secrecy of the response rests entirely on `si` never being
     revealed. That is the symbolic image of "the response is unpredictable
     without the device, and probing destroys it".
   - Helper data `hd` is a public value with no symbolic link to the response.
     This encodes Lemma 1 (helper-data privacy): the sketch discloses at most
     n-k bits, which the residual min-entropy argument plus the extractor turn
     into a key. Tamarin cannot reproduce that counting argument, so the model
     *assumes* its conclusion rather than proving it. The assumption is exactly
     the computational lemma, and it is cited as such in the paper.
   - Hash output length (SHA3-256 truncated to 160 bits versus SPONGENT-160) is
     invisible here: both are an ideal h/1. Collision resistance is a
     computational claim and is not carried by this model.
   - AEAD is the ideal symmetric-encryption operator: confidentiality and
     integrity are assumed, not derived.

  Long-term compromise is modelled explicitly, not assumed away. `Reveal_ltk`
  emits a device's silicon secret and `Reveal_gs_master` the ground-station
  master key; `Capture_device` emits the captured node's non-volatile memory,
  which is public identities, public helper data, and the per-pair credentials
  actually delivered to that node. Every agreement lemma is conditioned on the
  compromise that would legitimately defeat it, so the conditions are visible in
  the model rather than hidden in the prose.

  TWO THINGS THE IDEAL THEORY MAKES WEAKER THAN THE PAPER'S THEOREMS
  ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Both are consequences of the builtin equational theories, not of the protocol,
  and both are stated where they bite:

   - `diffie-hellman` is *ideal*: g^(ab) is reachable only by holding a or b, and
     no rule emits either ephemeral scalar. Session-key lemmas are therefore
     satisfied outright, and the disjunctions in `session_key_secrecy` and
     `forward_secrecy` are strict weakenings included to mirror the paper's
     conditioned statements. The real content of Theorems 4 and 5 -- that a
     post-session reveal does not unlock a past key because gap-CDH protects the
     erased scalars -- is the computational argument, not this model.
   - `symmetric-encryption` is the ideal operator. Ciphertext integrity is
     built in, so `p2_agreement_gs` cannot be falsified by manipulating the M4
     package. `p3_initiator_delegates_own_credential` recovers the missing piece
     by reasoning about the credential that is actually issued and consumed,
     which is what the paper's capture theorem needs.

  The peer lemmas were also repaired against the implementation: Phase 3 now
  keys on the credential the node was issued (a 3-place fact) rather than on a
  free variable, session keys carry their owner so the key-secrecy lemmas are
  meaningful, M4 carries the rotated identity inside the AEAD as the code does,
  and the type-tag lemma is stated as an ordering between the two peer
  acceptance facts instead of a role-swapped pseudo-exclusion that the protocol
  could never satisfy.
*/

theory UavPufAuth
begin

builtins: hashing, diffie-hellman, symmetric-encryption

functions: mac/2, kdf/2, puf/2

/* ======================================================================== */
/* Phase 1: enrollment, in a secure facility over a trusted channel          */
/* ======================================================================== */

rule Setup_gs_master:
  [ Fr(~mkgs) ]
  --[ ]->
  [ !Gsmk(~mkgs) ]

/*
  One device. `si` is the silicon secret; it is the only item that is neither
  transmitted nor stored in the ground-station database entry the model exports.
  `ch` is the public challenge block, `hd` the public helper data. All three
  public items are emitted, so the adversary starts out knowing everything a
  capture of this device would yield except the credentials.
*/
rule Enroll_device:
  [ Fr(~si), Fr(~tid), Fr(~ch), Fr(~hd) ]
  --[ Enrolled($D), LtkSecret($D, puf(~si, ~ch)) ]->
  [ !Dev($D, ~tid, ~si, ~ch, ~hd),
    !Db($D, ~tid, ~si, ~ch, ~hd),
    Out(<'enroll', ~tid, ~ch, ~hd>) ]

/*
  Pair credential. The implementation derives Cred_ij = KDF(mk_GS; pair-cred ||
  min(i,j) || max(i,j)), which is one secret per *unordered* pair. Tamarin has no
  ordering on names, so the rule publishes the same term in both orientations,
  which is the symbolic counterpart of the min/max canonicalisation. Each
  instance yields one credential; the mirrored instance yields a second, and a
  session that happens to mix them simply fails to verify its MAC, which is a
  liveness question and not a security one.
*/
rule Setup_pair_credential:
  [ !Gsmk(mkgs), !Db($A, ta, sa, ca, ha), !Db($B, tb, sb, cb, hb) ]
  --[ PairCred($A, $B, kdf(mkgs, <'paircred', $A, $B>)) ]->
  [ !Cred($A, $B, kdf(mkgs, <'paircred', $A, $B>)),
    !Cred($B, $A, kdf(mkgs, <'paircred', $A, $B>)) ]

/* ======================================================================== */
/* Phase 2: UAV <-> ground station                                          */
/* ======================================================================== */

rule P2_M1_uav_request:
  let mk = puf(si, ch)
      ka = kdf(mk, 'p2auth')
      ga = 'g'^~a
  in
  [ !Dev($I, tid, si, ch, hd), Fr(~a), Fr(~n1), Fr(~t1) ]
  --[ P2UavM1($I, ~n1, ga) ]->
  [ Out(<'m1', tid, ~n1, ~t1, ga, mac(ka, <'m1', tid, ~n1, ~t1, ga>)>),
    St2M1($I, tid, si, ch, ~a, ~n1, ~t1, ga) ]

rule P2_M2_gs_reply:
  let mk = puf(si, ch)
      ka = kdf(mk, 'p2auth')
      gb = 'g'^~b
  in
  [ !Db($I, tid, si, ch, hd),
    In(<'m1', tid, n1, t1, ga, mac(ka, <'m1', tid, n1, t1, ga>)>),
    Fr(~b), Fr(~n2), Fr(~t2) ]
  --[ P2GsReply($I, n1, ~n2, ga, gb) ]->
  [ Out(<'m2', ~n2, ~t2, gb, mac(ka, <'m2', tid, n1, ~n2, ~t2, ga, gb>)>),
    St2M2($I, tid, si, ch, ~b, n1, t1, ga, ~n2, ~t2, gb) ]

/*
  Verify-before-respond. The device emits M3 only when the incoming M2 carries a
  tag that verifies under ka, so a party that cannot compute ka elicits nothing.
  Note that the raw response never appears in either conclusion.
*/
rule P2_M3_uav_confirm:
  let mk = puf(si, ch)
      ka = kdf(mk, 'p2auth')
      tr = <'m1', tid, n1, t1, ga, 'm2', n2, t2, gb>
      sk = kdf(<mk, gb^a, <tid, n1, t1, ga, n2, t2, gb>>, 'p2sess')
  in
  [ St2M1($I, tid, si, ch, a, n1, t1, ga),
    In(<'m2', n2, t2, gb, mac(ka, <'m2', tid, n1, n2, t2, ga, gb>)>) ]
  --[ P2UavAccept($I, n1, n2, ga, gb), Secret($I, sk) ]->
  [ Out(<'m3', mac(ka, <'m3', tr>)>) ]

/*
  M4 carries the rotated identity inside the AEAD, as the implementation does
  (docs/spec-deviations.md item 2): the plaintext is <'credpkg', tid, cred, tid'>, so
  an attacker cannot desynchronise the identity without breaking the ciphertext.

  The rule also needs a peer device to exist, because a credential package is a
  package *of pairs*. That does not restrict the Phase-2 lemmas: those are
  universally quantified over the device, and `Enroll_device` may be applied any
  number of times, so a second device is always available to any candidate
  counterexample trace.
*/
rule P2_M4_gs_confirm:
  let mk = puf(si, ch)
      ka = kdf(mk, 'p2auth')
      tr = <'m1', tid, n1, t1, ga, 'm2', n2, t2, gb>
      sk = kdf(<mk, ga^b, <tid, n1, t1, ga, n2, t2, gb>>, 'p2sess')
  in
  [ St2M2($I, tid, si, ch, b, n1, t1, ga, n2, t2, gb),
    !Cred($I, $J, cred),
    !Db($J, tidj, sj, cj, hj),
    In(<'m3', mac(ka, <'m3', tr>)>) ]
  --[ P2GsAccept($I, n1, n2, ga, gb), Secret($I, sk),
      CredDelivered($I, $J, cred) ]->
  [ Out(<'m4', senc(<'credpkg', tid, cred, h(<tid, mk, n1, n2>)>, sk)>),
    !GotCred($I, $J, cred) ]

/* ======================================================================== */
/* Phase 3: UAV <-> UAV, no ground-station involvement                      */
/* ======================================================================== */

rule P3_P1_peer_request:
  let kaij = kdf(cred, 'peerauth')
      ga = 'g'^~x
  in
  [ !GotCred($I, $J, cred), Fr(~x), Fr(~ni), Fr(~tp) ]
  --[ P3Start($I, $J, ~ni, ga) ]->
  [ Out(<'p1', ~ni, ~tp, ga, mac(kaij, <'p1', $I, $J, ~ni, ~tp, ga>)>),
    St3P1($I, $J, cred, ~x, ~ni, ga) ]

/*
  The responder's acceptance is placed here, where it verifies the request, rather
  than at a fourth rule where it would consume the completion. The implementation
  derives the responder's session key when the completion arrives; the model
  derives it one step earlier, which only makes the key *available* sooner and
  therefore never strengthens a secrecy conclusion. Mutual authentication is
  unaffected: the responder authenticates the initiator when the request
  verifies, and the initiator authenticates the responder in the completion rule.
*/
rule P3_P2_peer_response:
  let kaij = kdf(cred, 'peerauth')
      gb = 'g'^~y
      sk = kdf(<cred, ga^y, <ni, ga, ~nj, gb>>, 'peersess')
  in
  [ !GotCred($J, $I, cred),
    In(<'p1', ni, tp, ga, mac(kaij, <'p1', $I, $J, ni, tp, ga>)>),
    Fr(~y), Fr(~nj) ]
  --[ P3PeerAccept($J, $I, ni, ~nj, ga, gb), Secret($J, sk) ]->
  [ Out(<'p2', ~nj, gb, mac(kaij, <'p2', $I, $J, ni, ~nj, ga, gb>)>) ]

rule P3_P3_peer_complete:
  let kaij = kdf(cred, 'peerauth')
      ta1 = mac(kaij, <'p1', $I, $J, ni, tp, ga>)
      sk = kdf(<cred, gb^x, <ni, ga, nj, gb>>, 'peersess')
  in
  [ St3P1($I, $J, cred, x, ni, ga),
    In(<'p2', nj, gb, mac(kaij, <'p2', $I, $J, ni, nj, ga, gb>)>) ]
  --[ P3InitAccept($I, $J, ni, nj, ga, gb), Secret($I, sk) ]->
  [ Out(<'p3', mac(kaij, <'p3', ta1, nj, gb>)>) ]

/* ======================================================================== */
/* Adversary capabilities                                                   */
/* ======================================================================== */

/*
  Physical capture: read non-volatile memory. Public identities, public helper
  data, and every pair credential actually delivered to this node are exposed.
  The silicon secret is not, and neither is any live session key: the key-secrecy
  theorem is stated for sessions whose endpoints are not corrupted, so a rule
  that handed over established keys would only restate its own hypothesis.
  Persistent `!GotCred` facts mean repeated applications extract every credential
  incident to the node, which is the N-1 exposure the paper states.
*/
rule Capture_device:
  [ !Dev($I, tid, si, ch, hd), !GotCred($I, $P, cred) ]
  --[ Corrupt($I) ]->
  [ Out(<'nvm', tid, ch, hd>), Out(<'cred', cred>) ]

/* Long-term reveal, for the forward-secrecy query. Emits the silicon secret. */
rule Reveal_ltk:
  [ !Dev($I, tid, si, ch, hd) ]
  --[ RevealLtk($I) ]->
  [ Out(<'silicon', si>) ]

rule Reveal_gs_master:
  [ !Gsmk(mkgs) ]
  --[ RevealGsmk() ]->
  [ Out(<'gsmk', mkgs>) ]

/* ======================================================================== */
/* Lemmas                                                                   */
/* ======================================================================== */

/*
  Theorem 1 (Phase-2 mutual authentication), device direction. If the device
  accepted a Phase-2 run, then the ground station really did emit the response
  the device accepted, with the same nonces and the same ephemeral shares. The
  condition is exactly the one under which this can fail: a revealed silicon
  secret lets the adversary compute the MAC key and forge M2. Replay cannot
  produce the same acceptance twice, because the device's own state is consumed
  when it sends M3.
*/
lemma p2_agreement_uav:
  "All I n1 n2 ga gb #i.
     P2UavAccept(I, n1, n2, ga, gb) @ i
   & not (Ex #r. RevealLtk(I) @ r)
   ==> (Ex #j. P2GsReply(I, n1, n2, ga, gb) @ j & #j < #i
        & not (Ex #k. P2GsReply(I, n1, n2, ga, gb) @ k & not (#k = #j)))"

/*
  Theorem 1, ground-station direction. The ground station accepts only a run
  whose confirmation message verifies under the key derived from the registered
  master key, so absent a reveal the device must have run the confirmation step,
  with the same transcript.
*/
lemma p2_agreement_gs:
  "All I n1 n2 ga gb #i.
     P2GsAccept(I, n1, n2, ga, gb) @ i
   & not (Ex #r. RevealLtk(I) @ r)
   ==> (Ex #j. P2UavAccept(I, n1, n2, ga, gb) @ j & #j < #i
        & not (Ex #k. P2UavAccept(I, n1, n2, ga, gb) @ k & not (#k = #j)))"

/*
  Theorem 2 (replay resistance). The ground station completes at most one Phase-2
  run per device nonce. A verbatim replay of M1 is therefore refused: it can
  produce a second reply, but never a second completion, because the confirmation
  the completion consumes is bound to the single stored run state.
*/
lemma p2_replay_unique:
  "All I n1 n2 ga gb n2b gb2 #i #j.
     P2GsAccept(I, n1, n2, ga, gb) @ i
   & P2GsAccept(I, n1, n2b, ga, gb2) @ j
   & not (Ex #r. RevealLtk(I) @ r)
   ==> #i = #j"

/*
  Theorem 3 (peer mutual authentication), initiator direction. The completion
  token the initiator accepts binds both identities, both nonces, and both
  ephemeral shares under the pair key, so it can only have come from the named
  peer holding the same credential.
*/
lemma p3_agreement_init:
  "All I J ni nj ga gb #i.
     P3InitAccept(I, J, ni, nj, ga, gb) @ i
   & not (Ex #r. Corrupt(I) @ r)
   & not (Ex #r. Corrupt(J) @ r)
   & not (Ex #r. RevealGsmk() @ r)
   ==> (Ex #j. P3PeerAccept(J, I, ni, nj, ga, gb) @ j & #j < #i
        & not (Ex #k. P3PeerAccept(J, I, ni, nj, ga, gb) @ k & not (#k = #j)))"

/*
  Theorem 3, responder direction: the responder answered a request that the
  named peer actually issued with the same nonce and share.
*/
lemma p3_agreement_resp:
  "All I J ni nj ga gb #i.
     P3PeerAccept(J, I, ni, nj, ga, gb) @ i
   & not (Ex #r. Corrupt(I) @ r)
   & not (Ex #r. Corrupt(J) @ r)
   & not (Ex #r. RevealGsmk() @ r)
   ==> (Ex #j. P3Start(I, J, ni, ga) @ j & #j < #i)"

/*
  Corollary 1 (no PUF read-out oracle). The adversary learns a device's PUF
  response only if it has taken that device's silicon secret. Nothing in the
  protocol transmits the response, and the ground-station database holds the
  derived key rather than the response itself, so a forged ground-station
  challenge is not a query to the PUF.
*/
lemma puf_response_secrecy:
  "All D x #i #j.
     LtkSecret(D, x) @ i & K(x) @ j
   ==> (Ex #r. RevealLtk(D) @ r)"

/*
  Theorem 4 (session-key secrecy). A session key becomes known only if a
  long-term secret has been revealed. Capture of a device's memory is not
  enough: the credentials it yields do not help against a key that also folds in
  the ephemeral Diffie-Hellman secret. Corrupting both endpoints of a session is
  outside the theorem, which is why this lemma is conditioned on reveal rather
  than on capture.

  One modelling point the paper's prose does not state, recorded here because it
  is what makes the lemma meaningful rather than vacuous. Tamarin's builtin
  diffie-hellman is the *ideal* theory: the adversary can form g^(ab) only by
  holding a or b, and neither ephemeral scalar is ever emitted. Consequently the
  session key is unreachable outright in this model, and the disjunction below is
  a strict weakening included to mirror the paper's conditioned statement. The
  fine-grained argument -- that a reveal alone does not recover the key because
  gap-CDH protects the erased scalars -- is the computational Theorem 4, not this
  lemma, exactly as the model header says.
*/
lemma session_key_secrecy:
  "All I x #i #j.
     Secret(I, x) @ i & K(x) @ j
   ==> (Ex #r. RevealLtk(I) @ r) | (Ex #r. RevealGsmk() @ r)"

/*
  Corollary 2 (delegation is credential-bound). A Phase 3 initiator emits its
  first message only under a credential it genuinely holds, and the credential
  names an unordered pair that includes the initiator. The two disjuncts are the
  two orientations of the one credential, so the fact is a single per-pair fact
  rather than two independent ones. This is the piece the ideal AEAD theory
  cannot supply on its own: it is what binds an accepted peer handshake to a
  credential the node was actually issued, so capturing a third node yields
  nothing with which to answer a pair that does not include it.
*/
lemma p3_initiator_delegates_own_credential:
  "All I J ni ga #i.
     P3Start(I, J, ni, ga) @ i
   ==> (Ex k c #j. PairCred(I, J, c) @ j & !GotCred(I, J, c) @ k)
     | (Ex k c #j. PairCred(J, I, c) @ j & !GotCred(I, J, c) @ k)
     | (Ex m. Corrupt(I) @ m & m < #i)"

/*
  Theorem 5 (perfect forward secrecy). This is Theorem 4 with the order of events
  pinned down: any long-term reveal that could explain a leaked session key must
  have happened before that session began. A reveal after a session closes does
  not help, because recovering the key would require the Diffie-Hellman secret
  from the recorded, authenticated shares, and the exchange is mandatory so there
  is no non-ephemeral fallback.

  Note the earlier version of this lemma left the revealed device as a free
  variable bound only by the premise, so a model with two reveals -- one before
  the session and one after -- would have falsified the ordering claim even where
  the key was leaked only by the earlier one. The owner is now fixed to the
  session owner I, which is the honest statement of what is being proved.
*/
lemma forward_secrecy:
  "All I x #i #j.
     Secret(I, x) @ i & K(x) @ j
   ==> (Ex #r. RevealLtk(I) @ r & #r < #i)
     | (Ex #r. RevealGsmk() @ r & #r < #i)"

/*
  Theorem 6 (physical capture resistance). A per-pair credential reaches the
  adversary only through capture of one of the two devices that hold it, or
  through the ground-station master key from which all credentials are derived.
  Capturing a third node is therefore of no use against a pair that does not
  involve it, which is the N-1 exposure bound.
*/
lemma credential_isolation:
  "All A B c #i #j.
     PairCred(A, B, c) @ i & K(c) @ j
   ==> (Ex #r. Corrupt(A) @ r) | (Ex #r. Corrupt(B) @ r)
     | (Ex #r. RevealGsmk() @ r)"

/*
  Theorem 2, peer half: no reflection. With distinct type tags under one key, the
  completion the initiator consumes ('p3') can only have been minted after a
  response ('p2'), which in turn can only follow a request ('p1'). Reflection
  would require the initiator's own request token to be accepted as the response,
  which would make the initiator's acceptance precede the responder's -- exactly
  the ordering this lemma forbids. The statement is therefore an ordering between
  the two acceptance facts, which is the form the perfect Dolev-Yao theory can
  actually test.

  The previous version (`P3InitAccept(I,J,...) implies not
  P3PeerAccept(I,J,...)`) was unsound: it swapped the roles, so the second fact
  was not the reflection of the first at all (that would be P3PeerAccept(J,I,...)).
  It asserted a property of a fact the protocol never produces and would have
  passed for the wrong reason.
*/
lemma p3_type_tag_separation:
  "All I J ni nj ga gb #i #k.
     P3InitAccept(I, J, ni, nj, ga, gb) @ i
   & P3PeerAccept(J, I, ni, nj, ga, gb) @ k
   & not (Ex #r. Corrupt(I) @ r)
   & not (Ex #r. Corrupt(J) @ r)
   & not (Ex #r. RevealGsmk() @ r)
   ==> #k < #i"

end
